<h2 id="care-two-things">A pension and a care service are different things</h2>

<dl>
  <dt>A pension payment</dt>
  <dd>Cash, paid to a person every 4 weeks, for as long as they live. The person decides how to spend it. The amount follows set rules about a person's National Insurance record. It does not depend on need.</dd>
  <dt>A social care service</dt>
  <dd>Help with washing, dressing, eating and getting about, at home or in a care home. It goes only to people assessed as needing it, when they need it. Many people never use it. Some need a great deal.</dd>
</dl>

<p>So a change that slows the growth of the pension for pensioners in general, and gives a service to some, is not an exchange of equal value for each person. It moves money from pensioners in general to people who need care. Whether that is fair is a judgement. Who gains and who loses is a question of fact that depends on the design.</p>

<h2 id="care-scope">Who each policy covers: the State Pension is UK-wide, social care is not</h2>

<p>The State Pension is paid across the UK. Social care is run separately by each nation.</p>

{{ m.dataTable("care-by-nation", false) }}

<p>The commission on adult social care is for England.</p>

{% call m.note("Why this matters: the two policies cannot yet be treated as covering the same people", 3, "why-this-matters-the-two-policies-cannot-yet-be-treated-as-c") %}
<p>In our reading, the pension change would affect pensioners in all four nations, because the State Pension is paid across the UK. Northern Ireland sets its rates through its own orders, which we did not read.</p>
<p>{{ ("no-ncs-nations" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("no-ncs-nations") }} Scotland already provides free personal care. <a href="#care-now">Social care now, nation by nation</a>. Until the government says how each nation is treated, the two policies cannot be treated as covering the same people.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="care-now">Social care now, nation by nation</h2>

<p>Each nation runs its own system. England means-tests care with no lifetime limit on what a person pays, Scotland provides free personal care to people assessed as needing it, Wales caps the charge for care at home, and Northern Ireland means-tests care home fees.</p>

<h3 id="england">England</h3>
<p>Care is means-tested. A person with capital above {{ m.f("care-eng-upper-limit") }} pays the full cost. Below {{ m.f("care-eng-lower-limit") }}, capital is ignored. There is no limit on what a person can pay over a lifetime.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("care-eng-upper-limit" | figure) }}

<h3 id="scotland">Scotland</h3>
<p>Personal and nursing care are free for people assessed as needing them. For a person in a care home who pays their own fees, the payments are {{ m.f("care-scot-personal") }} a week for personal care and {{ m.f("care-scot-nursing") }} for nursing care. They do not cover accommodation.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("care-scot-personal" | figure) }}

<h3 id="wales">Wales</h3>
<p>The most a person can be charged for care at home is {{ m.f("care-wales-max-charge") }} a week. For residential care the capital limit is {{ m.f("care-wales-residential-limit") }}. For care at home a different figure applies, {{ m.f("care-wales-home-limit") }}.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("care-wales-residential-limit" | figure) }}

<h3 id="northern-ireland">Northern Ireland</h3>
<p>Care home fees are means-tested, with an upper capital limit of {{ m.f("care-ni-upper-limit") }}.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("care-ni-upper-limit" | figure) }}

<h3 id="cash-benefits-for-care-needs">Cash benefits for care needs</h3>
<p>Attendance Allowance is a cash benefit from the UK government, paid in England and Wales. It is not a care service and is not means-tested. {{ m.c("aa-scotland") }}</p>

<h2 id="care-scale">The sums involved</h2>

<p>The saving from the pension change starts small and builds up over decades, and the care service is due to start in phases in the next Parliament, so its early years are the years when the saving is smallest.</p>

<p>These figures are from different sources, for different years and on different price bases. They are set out to show scale. They must not be added or subtracted as if they were one account.</p>

