<h3 id="scale-saving">Estimated saving from the pension change</h3>
<p>The government estimates a saving of {{ m.f("saving-rule-2030-late-2030s") }} in {{ ("saving-rule-2030-late-2030s" | figure).period_label }} and {{ m.f("saving-rule-2030-2050") }} in {{ ("saving-rule-2030-2050" | figure).period_label }}, in cash. {{ ("ifs-savings-small-early" | claim).statement }} The estimates and their bases are in <a href="/reform-options/costs/#saving-2030">The rule announced for April 2030</a>.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("saving-rule-2030-late-2030s" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("What the scale shows, and what it does not", 3, "what-the-scale-shows-and-what-it-does-not") %}
<p>The saving starts in April 2030 and builds up slowly. The care service is due to start in the next Parliament, in phases. The government says the service will grow as the saving grows, and will not be paid for by borrowing. It has not said what the service costs in each year, or what pays for it if the saving falls short.</p>
<p>The government's figure for 2039 to 2040 and the Health Foundation's figures are for different years. The Health Foundation's are in the prices of 2023/24 and cover England. The government's is in cash, or in the prices of 2025 to 2026, and covers Great Britain. So the two cannot be compared closely.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="care-questions">Ten questions, and what the announcement answers</h2>

<p>These ten questions were written before the announcement, as the test for any such plan. Each now shows what the announcement says.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>What rule would replace the triple lock, from when, and for whom?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> The rule and the date are given. {{ ("dwp-2030-coverage" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("dwp-2030-coverage") }} {{ ("dwp-2030-other-parts" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("dwp-2030-other-parts") }} Neither the announcement nor the department says which measure of earnings would be used. The announcement's one example refers to 2030/31.</li>
  <li><strong>What care would be covered?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> {{ ("ncs-personal-care" | claim).statement }} {{ ("ncs-not-bed-board" | claim).statement }} It does not say whether care at home and care in a care home are treated the same.</li>
  <li><strong>Who would be eligible?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> Older people, based on need. No age, no level of need and no nation is given.</li>
  <li><strong>Who loses income, who receives a service, and when?</strong> <span class="status">Not answered.</span> {{ ("no-rule-2030-distribution" | claim).statement }}</li>
  <li><strong>What are the costs and savings, and against what baseline?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> The Department for Work and Pensions gives savings for two years, measured against keeping the triple lock, in cash and in 2025 to 2026 prices. It does not give the paths of earnings and prices behind them. {{ ("no-ncs-cost" | claim).statement }}</li>
  <li><strong>Would the saving actually be used for care?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> The government says it will be. {{ ("ncs-savings-used" | claim).note }}</li>
  <li><strong>What pays for the early years?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> The service is to be phased in as the saving grows, and not paid for by borrowing. {{ ("minister-more-funding" | claim).statement }}</li>
  <li><strong>Can the service be delivered?</strong> <span class="status">Answered in part.</span> The announcement says the service will grow as staff and providers are built up. It gives no plan for that. {{ ("workforce-plan-promised" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("workforce-plan-promised") }} The Prime Minister's Office said the commission would report in summer 2027. The commission's published terms still say 2026 for the first phase and by 2028 for the second. See <a href="#status-law">In law or in a government document</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>What happens in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland?</strong> <span class="status">Not answered.</span> {{ ("no-ncs-nations" | claim).statement }} The Scottish Government has put figures on how much less pensioners in Scotland could receive: <a href="#status-scotgov">What the Scottish Government said</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>What happens to people on the lowest incomes?</strong> <span class="status">Not answered.</span> {{ ("no-rule-2030-pension-credit" | claim).statement }} {{ ("dwp-2030-poverty" | claim).statement }} It puts the share at {{ m.f("dwp-2030-poverty-2025") }} in 2024 to 2025 and {{ m.f("dwp-2030-poverty-2050") }} in 2049 to 2050. {{ m.c("dwp-2030-poverty") }} {{ ("dwp-2030-poverty-conditional" | claim).statement }} Our reading: {{ ("dwp-2030-poverty-not-effect" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("dwp-2030-poverty-not-effect") }} In a speech the Prime Minister separately said pensioners on low incomes would not pay Income Tax in this Parliament. {{ m.c("pm-speech-income-tax") }} The speech is as published by the Labour Party. GOV.UK carries no transcript. That is about tax, not the rule. See <a href="/state-pension/#tax-and-payment">tax and the State Pension</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Each answer rests on the government's announcement, unless another source is named above. {{ m.c("rule-2030") }}</p>

<h2 id="care-not-assume">What should not be assumed</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>That the change is law.</strong> It is an announcement, with legislation promised.</li>
  <li><strong>That the saving is there when the care service starts.</strong> <a href="#care-scale">The sums involved</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>That each person who gives up pension income receives care of the same value.</strong> <a href="#care-two-things">A pension and a care service are different things</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>That changing the rule means a cut in pounds.</strong> {{ ("rule-2030-no-fall" | claim).statement }}</li>
  <li><strong>That a think tank's estimate is the cost of the government's plan.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>That a name describes a rule.</strong> The government calls the rule an adjusted triple lock. Some news reports call it a double lock. <a href="/reform-options/#opt-2030">What the rule does</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>{{ ("hf-affordable" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("hf-affordable") }} That is a view about priorities. Others weigh the same figures differently.</p>

